    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the codebase's established conventions and adds nothing that becomes a catch-all.\n\n- **`RequireEmailNotDisposable`** is a textbook peer of the existing `Require*` guard functions (`RequireRegistrationDateNotFuture`, etc.): one public `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto)` method, throws a checked escalation, skips blank input. Idiomatic placement and shape.\n- **`DisposableEmailDomains`** deliberately parallels `EmailNormalizer` \u2014 package-private `final` helper, private constructor, same \"single definition\u2026 kept out of the function classes because OfficeFloor requires one public method\" rationale in the javadoc. Keeping the blocklist + domain-matching as its own cohesive helper (rather than piling it into `EmailNormalizer` or the function class) is the right unit and matches the existing split of concerns.\n- **`DisposableEmailDomainException` + `DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler`** are near-exact structural mirrors of `InvalidEmailException`/`InvalidEmailExceptionHandler`, down to the `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}` body shape (which the handler's own javadoc calls out). The codebase already establishes one exception+handler pair per validation concern, so this is consistent, not duplicative.\n- The YAML wiring (new `disposableEmail` step inserted after `email`/`NormalizeOwnerEmail`, sharing `govern: [ transaction ]`, plus the escalation file named by FQCN) follows the documented endpoint pattern, and running after normalization is the sensible ordering.\n\nNo duplication of existing logic (no prior domain-extraction helper exists), no misplaced responsibilities, and a maintainer of this repo would find each new file exactly where they'd expect its analogue to live.",
